"""Internal activation workflow of the ``*_apply`` modules; not a public interface.

An activation module compares saved with loaded state per object, applies
when an object drifts or when asked to, and verifies afterwards. It runs on
the lifecycle base, so the binding's connection, compatibility policy and
failure contract apply: :class:`Activation` holds the sequence, an apply
module supplies the product steps as hooks, and :func:`run` serves a
subsystem whose steps are plain functions of the client.

``verify`` names the objects to compare, as names or as items with a
``name`` and an optional ``uuid``. A subsystem whose comparison covers every
object it owns (``owned``) also accepts an empty ``verify``. The module
applies when a compared object drifts, also when one cannot be verified if
``apply_unverifiable`` is set, and always with ``activate: always``.

A subsystem whose product tracks pending work follows it (``pending``): it
applies when work is pending, reports pending objects as drifting, and
refuses drift without pending work before applying, because the apply would
not repair it. After the apply only the pending objects are verified. A
subsystem whose comparison and pending work read the same state first reads
it once in ``survey``, which runs before the first comparison.

A reply lost after the apply request was sent (a timeout, a dropped
connection or an undecodable body) proves nothing by itself. The module goes
on to verify only when an object drifted before: its convergence shows that
the apply ran, and a drifting object that stays out of sync fails as not
effective. An object that stays unverifiable leaves the lost reply
unresolved, so the module fails with it and the outcome unknown. Without
any drift, for example ``activate: always`` on objects already in sync, the
module fails with the outcome unknown and never sends the apply again.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

